Yes. Ubuntu 22.04 ships 3.22, the project wants 3.28. Install a newer one via pip inside the container:

bash
pip3 install --user "cmake>=3.28,<4"
export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"
hash -r
cmake --version

The <4 bound matters. CMake 4.x is what caused problems on the host, since it rejects the old cmake_minimum_required declarations inside LLVM's subprojects. You want something in the 3.28 to 3.31 range.

Make it stick for future shells:

bash
echo 'export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"' >> ~/.bashrc

Then rebuild:

bash
rm -rf ~/work/ascend/AscendNPU-IR/build-ubuntu
cd ~/work/ascend/AscendNPU-IR
bash build-tools/build.sh -o build-ubuntu -j 32

Confirm cmake reports 3.28 or higher before starting, otherwise the script may still find the system one at /usr/bin/cmake.
